Interpretation of Logistic Regression Model:
Logistic regression predicts probability using this function:
p = 1 / 1+e−(β0+β1x1+β2x2+...+βnxn)
=>Each coefficient (β) shows how much a feature affects the log-odds of diabetes.
=>Positive coefficient → increases chance of diabetes
=>Negative coefficient → decreases chance of diabetes

Example Interpretation:
=>Glucose has the highest positive coefficient → Strongest predictor of diabetes
=>BMI also has a positive coefficient → Higher BMI increases diabetes risk
=>Age → Older individuals are more likely to be diabetic
=>Features with small/negative coefficients → weaker or inverse relationship

Clear Model Evaluation Interpretation:
=>Accuracy → Overall correctness of model
=>Precision → Out of predicted diabetic patients, how many are actually diabetic
=>Recall → Out of actual diabetic patients, how many were correctly identified
=>F1 Score → Balance between precision and recall
The model achieved an accuracy of X%, indicating good overall performance. The recall score is particularly important in this case, as identifying diabetic patients correctly is critical. A higher recall ensures fewer false negatives, which is essential in healthcare applications.

